# Line-ending policy: store ALL text files as LF in the repository.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text vs binary and normalize text to LF on commit
# (a Windows working tree still checks out CRLF when core.autocrlf=true, but the
# repo always holds LF). Putting it here makes the policy repo-enforced and
# portable, rather than depending on each contributor's `core.autocrlf` setting.
* text=auto

# ─── Explicit text types (stored as LF) ──────────────────────────────────────
*.rs     text
*.toml   text
*.lock   text
*.md     text
*.json   text
*.py     text
*.ld     text
*.conf   text
*.gsh    text
*.gs     text

# ─── Scripts that MUST stay LF even on a Windows checkout ─────────────────────
*.sh     text eol=lf

# ─── BOOT CONFIGS, read by a BOOTLOADER rather than by this machine ───────────
#
# Same class as `*.sh` above and for a sharper reason: nothing here is ever read by
# Windows. `*.conf text` alone means "normalize to LF on commit, convert to NATIVE on
# checkout" - and native on a Windows checkout is CRLF. The repo held LF, the working
# tree held CRLF, and `Copy-Item` put CRLF on the card.
#
# U-Boot's extlinux parser then treated the trailing `\r` as part of the FILENAME and
# every entry failed with `Failed to load '/godspeed-riscv64-visionfive.img'` - while
# the MENU still rendered perfectly, because a trailing `\r` in a display string just
# returns the cursor and is invisible. That cost two card reflashes and two wrong
# theories (`backlog/26`).
#
# The boot directory is the whole rule rather than a list of extensions, because the
# next board will bring a file type nobody thought to add.
boot/**   text eol=lf

# ─── Binary: never normalize line endings, never diff as text ─────────────────
*.img    binary
*.bin    binary
*.elf    binary
*.efi    binary
*.iso    binary
*.o      binary
*.a      binary
*.png    binary
*.bmp    binary
*.ppm    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
